WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.480
What makes a critical-section solution

2
00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.720
correct? We need more than

3
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.360
a sign saying only one

4
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.780
process may enter. The protocol

5
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.640
must address mutual exclusion, progress

6
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.660
and bounded waiting as distinct

7
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.180
requirements, each supported by its

8
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:16.800
own argument.

9
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.860
The critical section accesses shared

10
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.140
data whose conflicting use must

11
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.020
be coordinated. Entry code asks

12
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.220
permission, exit code releases it,

13
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.040
and the remainder is outside

14
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.940
that protected work. A correct

15
00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.740
protocol must be judged under

16
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.540
its stated assumptions about execution

17
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:34.720
and atomic access.

18
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.120
Mutual exclusion says that when

19
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.440
one process is in its

20
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.720
critical section, another cannot be

21
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.020
there simultaneously. This is a

22
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.040
safety claim about forbidden overlap.

23
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.120
Show the variable or ownership

24
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.880
rule that makes two simultaneous

25
00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.000
entries impossible.

26
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.080
Progress asks whether contenders can

27
00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.040
select a next entrant when

28
00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.600
the critical section is empty.

29
00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.480
A process not trying to

30
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.540
enter must not indefinitely prevent

31
00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.780
another from entering. This is

32
00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.220
not the same as checking

33
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.380
that two processes never overlap.

34
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.400
In strict alternation, turn zero

35
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.040
allows P zero to enter

36
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.480
and turn one allows P

37
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.200
one. Exclusion holds because only

38
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.500
the selected turn can pass

39
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.940
the guard. But if turn

40
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.360
stays zero while P zero

41
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.000
remains in its remainder section,

42
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:22.640
P one can be stuck

43
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.520
despite an empty critical section.

44
00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.800
The course separately considers a

45
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.820
bound on overtaking under alternation,

46
00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:30.520
but that does not repair

47
00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.760
its progress failure. A bound

48
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.240
on how often another process

49
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.120
enters is not evidence that

50
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.740
an uninterested owner will ever

51
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.980
change turn. Explain the failing

52
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.060
scenario rather than presenting three

53
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.060
unexplained ticks.

54
00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.800
The flags-only attempt lets each

55
00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.380
process announce interest before waiting

56
00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.100
on the other flag. If

57
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.740
P zero sets its flag

58
00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.180
and P one sets its

59
00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.080
flag before either checks, both

60
00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.180
see the other interested. They

61
00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.880
can then spin forever while

62
00:01:58.880 --> 00:01:59.920
nobody is inside.

63
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.380
Bounded waiting limits how many

64
00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:04.340
times other processes may enter

65
00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.380
after a particular request and

66
00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.940
before it is granted. A

67
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:09.760
solution can allow some progress

68
00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.160
while repeatedly favoring one contender,

69
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.200
so progress alone does not

70
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.220
imply bounded waiting. Queue discipline

71
00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:17.880
and tie resolution can matter.

72
00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.140
A useful proof style is

73
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.960
to separate the properties. Give

74
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.520
an exclusion invariant, explain how

75
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.320
an empty section admits some

76
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.340
interested process, then bound overtaking

77
00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.480
for a particular requester. If

78
00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.420
the memory model or atomic-access

79
00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.040
assumptions change, revisit the proof.

80
00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:37.780
Things to take note of.

81
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.380
Strict alternation can fail progress,

82
00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.280
and flags alone can make

83
00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.700
both contenders wait. Neither a

84
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.780
safe-looking lock variable nor a

85
00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.620
fair processor schedule is a

86
00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:50.280
complete proof of all three

87
00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.680
critical-section requirements.
